# castle.dlg — every word the twelve say, and what moves them to say it.
#
# Generated by `npm run dialogue:extract` and read back by
# `npm run dialogue:compile`. `npm run dialogue:check` exits non-zero if this
# file and data/ have drifted apart, and test/dialogue.mjs runs the same check.
#
#   @ id | name | role | ward     a speaker, in data/npcs.json cast order
#   : state                       one of that speaker's dialogue states
#   ? what moves them into it     a press, or a quest stage
#   % the quest stage objective   only under a `? quest` line
#   ! what the state grants       a clue sourced on it
#   | one line of dialogue        verbatim, in order
#   # a comment                   ignored
#
# ONLY `|` AND `%` ARE WRITTEN BACK. The `@`, `:`, `?` and `!` lines are the
# clue graph, which lives in data/mystery.json and data/quests/ and is edited
# there; compile rebuilds them from those files and refuses if what is below
# has drifted from what they say. So: to add a state, wire its press or its
# quest stage first, re-run extract, and write the lines into the stub that
# appears here. To change a line or an objective, change it here and compile.

@ constable | Sir Roger Lestrange | Constable | inner
: default
! says constable-accident
| A clerk. A day early. Well, you have seen him, so you know what there is to know: the mason was drunk, the stair is steep, and he fell.
| The King's inspector rides in tomorrow. I want a clean sheet by Vespers, and I want the mason buried before the inspector's horse is watered.
| Ask what you must. Then come and tell me it was a fall.
| {ACCUSE}

@ steward | Piers Marrable | Steward | inner
: default
! says steward-denies
| The mason? A sad business. He drank, you know. They all do.
| I sent no summons. Why would I? The chapel roof is sound, I have the ledger to say so.
| If you want the works, the Clerk keeps the works. I keep the household.
: admits
? press default on summons-is-stewards
! says steward-admits
| Where did you get that? No. Do not tell me.
| I sent him to be talked to. By Robert. There was a matter of figures, and Robert wished to settle it like gentlemen.
| To be talked to. Not to the stair. I never meant the stair.

@ clerk | Master Robert Ferrour | Clerk of Works | outer
: default
! says clerk-abed
| Hywel. Yes. The best setter of stone in the March, and he could not set his own feet on a stair. A great loss to the works.
| I was abed in my lodging from Compline. My boy will tell you. I heard of it at first light with everyone else.
| The works are in order, if that is your errand. The lead is sheet-perfect. Count it yourself.
: cloak
? press default on wax-matches
! says clerk-cloak
| My cloak? That cloak has been in the laundry since Sunday. Ask the girl.
| Tallow is tallow. There are a hundred candles in this castle and I have brushed past most of them.
: cornered
? press default cloak on lead-sold
! says clerk-cornered
| Wastage. Yes. Wastage is a mason's word, clerk, not a thief's. Sheets crack. Sheets are cut short. Sheets are lost off a roof in a wind.
| You have a ledger and an apprentice's memory. The Constable has a stair and a drunk. Ask yourself which he will write down.

@ porter | Gwilym | Porter of the cross-wall gate | inner
: default
! says porter-log
! says porter-barred
| Every soul through this gate goes in the book, clerk. Compline: the mason, on the Steward's word, about the chapel roof. Nobody else, in or out.
| Gate barred behind him. And the walk door at my tower, barred as always.
| You want to know how a man in the outer ward got to a stair in the inner? He did not. That is what the book is for.
: admits
? press default on door-unbarred
! says porter-admits
| That door. God help me. I was in my cups, clerk, and it has not been barred this month, nor last.
| You know what that is? A hanging matter, to say so. So I say it is barred, and I have said it so long I half believe it.

@ cook | Marged | Cook | outer
: default
? quest cooks-knife at unheard
% Nobody has said anything about a knife.
! says cook-lantern
! says knife-missing
| Hywel took my lantern. The good one, off the kitchen hook, at Compline, and I told him bring it back and he said in the morning.
| Called to the inner ward, he said. The chapel roof. At that hour. I said the roof will keep and he said the Steward would not.
| And now I am short a lantern and a knife both. A knife gone since yesterday, off this table, and nobody knows a thing.
: knife-hunting
? quest cooks-knife at hunting
% Marged is short a boning knife, gone off her table since yesterday. Find it.
| A boning knife, a hand long, the haft bound with cord where it split. I would know it off any table in this castle.
| Look in the bakehouse before you look at me. Everything that walks out of this kitchen walks that way first.
| And the lantern. I have not forgotten the lantern, clerk, whatever else you find.
: knife-found
? quest cooks-knife at found
% The knife is in the bakehouse with flour on it. Go back and tell Marged.
| The bakehouse. Of course the bakehouse. Flour on it, was there?
| That is the boy. He takes it for the dough and leaves it in the trough and swears on his mother he never touched it.
| So I am short a lantern and not a knife. And the lantern is the one that went up the Chapel Tower stair. Ask me about the lantern.
: knife-settled
? quest cooks-knife at settled
% The knife is back on its hook. The lantern is still out there.
| The knife is on its hook and the boy has had what I had for him.
| My lantern is still out there, and the man who carried it off is laid out in the chapel.
| Ask me about that one as often as you like.

@ chaplain | Father Anselm | Chaplain | inner
: default
? quest candle-count at unlit
% Nobody has counted a candle.
| I found him with the Constable at first light. I have said the office for him. There is nothing more I can do for him now.
| My chamber is above the stair. I sleep sound, clerk. I heard nothing.
| Sir Roger says a fall. Sir Roger is the King's Constable.
| And since you are the King's clerk and count things for a living: I am a candle short. Four tallow in the aumbry at Compline, three at Lauds. It is not a matter. I mention it.
: candle-short
? quest candle-count at short
% Father Anselm's aumbry held four tallow candles at Compline and three at Lauds. Find the fourth.
| Four tallow, of the kitchen's making, in the aumbry by the vestry door, and the aumbry has no lock because who would rob a chapel of tallow. Three this morning.
| I keep the account for the exchequer's man. A candle a week for the office and one over for a burial. He will not care for one candle. I care for a column that does not add.
| I did not take it up to my chamber; I have said I sleep sound. Look on the stair if you like. I do not use the stair after Compline, and I have not looked.
: candle-found
? quest candle-count at found
% A tallow candle in the pricket at the first turn of the stair, and wax pooled on the step below it. Tell the chaplain his column adds.
| In the pricket at the first turn. That pricket has stood empty since Sir Walter's day. The office is said below, and nobody carries a light past the turn but me.
| Then my four is four, and somebody stood at the turn of my stair long enough to pool a candle's worth of tallow on the step. That is not a moment. That is a man waiting.
| Enter it in your column, clerk. I have my own to keep, and it adds now, which I find is not what I wanted.
: candle-entered
? quest candle-count at entered
% The chaplain's account adds: four candles, and one of them burnt down on his own stair.
| The column adds. One tallow, taken up the stair by a hand not mine, burnt to the socket. I have written it so.
| I have said the office for him twice now. I find I have not finished saying it.
| If you have anything the Steward has said that you would put to me, put it. I have been a coward once this week and I would rather not be asked to be one twice.
: heard
? press default on steward-admits
! says chaplain-feet
| The Steward said that? Then God forgive me, I will say what I heard.
| Two men on the stair at the second bell. Voices, low, then not low. Then one man, going up. Only one.
| I lay and listened to one man go up my stair and I did not get out of my bed. That is what I heard nothing means.

@ sentry | Dafydd ap Rhys | Garrison, night watch on the north walk | outer
: default
? quest sentrys-dice at square
% Nobody has mentioned dice.
! says sentry-sighting
| North walk, Compline to Lauds, every night this month. You see the whole north side from the Kitchen Tower door.
| Second bell, a man went east along the walk past my post. Fur-trimmed cloak. I know fur when the moon is on it.
| The Clerk's the only man in the works with fur on him. I thought he was walking off a supper. I wish I had stopped him.
| And since you go through the gate and I do not: Gwilym has four pence of mine at dice, and he has stopped playing and started asking. Tell him Saturday. Tell him I said it civil.
: dice-owed
? quest sentrys-dice at owed
% Dafydd owes Gwilym the porter four pence at dice. Tell the porter: Saturday, and civil.
| Four pence. It was two, then a throw for the two, and it was four. His dice, his lodge, his ale, at Lauds when the watch is done and the gate opens. I have not been through that lodge door since, nor will I till I have the four.
| He sits in there all day and all night with the book and the bar and the jug, and he does not forget a penny, that man, whatever else he forgets.
| Saturday. Say it so he hears it, clerk. He is a hard man to make hear a thing he does not want to.
: dice-told
? quest sentrys-dice at told
% The porter has been told. Go back to the north walk and tell Dafydd he heard it.
| You told him. And he said nothing, I would wager. That is Gwilym. He writes a thing down and says nothing.
| I will pay him Saturday and I will not sit at his table again, not at Lauds nor any other hour. That is my word and you may write it in your book.
| You have been in his lodge, then, and seen him with the jug. Then you know what a bar looks like to a man who has been at the jug since Compline. I say no more than that.
: dice-bar
? quest sentrys-dice at bar
% Gwilym has said it about the walk door. Dafydd has something to say about that too.
| He has said it, then. About the bar. God help him, and I say that meaning it.
| It has leaned on that wall since Lammas. Every man who walks the north walk has seen it lean there and every man of us has said the door is Gwilym's and not ours.
| And I owed him four pence and held my tongue about it, and you may write that against me too. A man went east past my post at the second bell and that door was open at the far end of his walk.
: dice-paid
? quest sentrys-dice at paid
% Saturday, and civil. Dafydd will pay his four pence and keep to his own walk.
| Saturday, and civil, and four pence he will have, and then I keep to my own walk and he keeps to his lodge.
| North walk, Compline to Lauds, from the Kitchen Tower door. That is my post and I was at it.
| Second bell, a man in a fur-trimmed cloak went east along it past me. I have said that to you already and I will say it to whoever asks.

@ apprentice | Ieuan | Hywel's apprentice | outer
: default
? quest hywels-chisel at untouched
% Nobody has spoken of tools.
! says hywel-sober
! says apprentice-tallies
| Drunk? Hywel? Not on a working day, clerk, and yesterday was one. Not a drop after the Sunday bell and not a drop till Saturday.
| He kept his own count of the lead. Eleven tally sticks on his belt, a notch a sheet. Two hundred and twelve laid, by his count, and he was going to show somebody.
| He went out of the lodge at Compline with the sticks on him. I know because he told me mind the fire.
| His tools lie where he left them, that is the custom, and I am the one that takes them up. Only the good chisel is not among them. Madoc had it for a new edge, and Madoc is in the tower, and nobody will let a lodge boy by the bars to ask him.
: chisel-asking
? quest hywels-chisel at asking
% Hywel's good chisel went to Madoc the smith for a new edge before Madoc was put in the Prison Tower. Ask Madoc where it is.
| Ash haft, a hand and a half long, his mark on the tang. He cut every head in the chapel with it. Nobody has had it in hand in four years but him and me, and me only to carry it.
| Madoc draws the lodge's chisels twice a year for ale, that is the custom, and that one went to him a week since with an edge on it like a spoon. Then they took him up the tower and the forge went cold.
| You are the King's man. They will let you by the bars. Ask him where he left it. That is all I want. I will finish the head he was on, and then I will lay my course at Michaelmas as he said I would.
: chisel-heard
? quest hywels-chisel at heard
% You have been to the bars. Tell Ieuan what the smith said, which was nothing about a chisel.
| You went in to him. And he gave you the lead and the cart and the Clerk's seal, and not one word about a chisel. That is Madoc. He has said the same three things to every man who has stood at those bars for eleven days.
| Then it is in the tower with him or wherever he put it, and I will not wait on it.
| There is a stone in the lodge and I have watched him draw an edge two years running. Mine will not be his. It will cut.
: chisel-forge
? quest hywels-chisel at forge
% Madoc is at the forge by day, under the Prison Tower wall. The chisel is where he works and not where he sleeps. Tell Ieuan.
| Out to the forge at Prime and in at Vespers, eight days of the eleven, and the roll says so? Then it never went up the tower at all. It is at the forge, on the bench or in the trough, where he works and not where he sleeps.
| Nobody stops a lodge boy at a forge. I will be there at Prime tomorrow when they walk him down, and he will hand it me over the wall as he has a hundred times.
| You had that off a barrel-head in the guardroom, and not one man of the garrison thought to read it in eleven days. The lodge can count. It seems the lodge can read as well.
: chisel-own-edge
? quest hywels-chisel at own-edge
% Ieuan has put his own edge on the second chisel. He will finish the head Hywel was on.
| I put an edge on the second chisel myself, on the lodge stone, and it took me till the bell. It is not Madoc's edge. It cuts.
| The head he was on is a king's, for the springing of the chapel arch. I will finish it as he began it and set the small cross under it as he did, and the chaplain need not know.
| He told me mind the fire. I have minded it. The rest I am learning.
: chisel-fetched
? quest hywels-chisel at fetched
% Prime tomorrow, at the forge: Ieuan will have Hywel's chisel off Madoc over the wall, as he has a hundred times.
| Prime tomorrow, at the forge, and I will have it off him. I have his mark on the tang and the custom on my side, and now the roll, which is the King's.
| I will finish the head he was on with his own iron. That is how it should be done, and I had given it up.
| He told me mind the fire. I have minded it.

@ laundress | Nest | Laundress | outer
: default
! says laundress-cloak
| That cloak? The Clerk's. His boy brought it down at first light and said wash it and say nothing.
| It goes in the copper at Sext with the rest. If you want to look at it, look now.
: wife
? press default on prisoner-story
! says nest-is-wife
| Madoc is my husband. Yes. Since you ask what nobody else has.
| They put him in the tower for the lead because somebody had to go in the tower. I have no reason on this earth to lie for the Clerk of Works, and one very good one to lie against him.
| So weigh what I say however you like. The cloak is still the Clerk's.

@ prisoner | Madoc the smith | Held for the lead theft | outer
: default
! says prisoner-story
| Another one come to look at the thief. Look, then.
| The lead went out the gate on a cart, by daylight, with the Clerk's seal on the pass. I saw it go. I said so. That is why I am here.
| Nobody believes a smith in a tower. You will not either. Go on.
: forge
? press default on prisoner-inside
! says prisoner-forge
| You have had the roll off the barrel-head. Somebody in this castle can read, then.
| Eight days at the forge and I watched that cart both ways. It came in empty and it went out with the axle riding low, and I have shod that horse eleven years. It was not stone.
| And the dates are in that roll and they have been in it for eleven days. Show it to the Constable. He will tell you a smith is a smith and a roll is a roll.

@ merchant | Thomas Wykes | Town merchant | outer
: default
! says merchant-stone
| Wykes, of the town. I have the cart contract for the works.
| Dressed stone. I buy stone off the works and sell it on. That is the whole of my trade, clerk.
| The cart? Sacking and stone. Mind your boots.
: admits
? press default on merchant-cart
! says merchant-admits
| You have been under the sacking. Well.
| The Clerk sells. The Steward signs the pass. I only buy, clerk, and a man who only buys has hanged for less, so I will be in the town by Sext and you may tell them I said so.

@ lady | Lady Alys | The Constable's wife | inner
: default
? quest ladys-hawk at perched
% Nobody has said anything about a bird.
! says lady-hand
| The King's clerk, in my chamber, about a mason. My husband will be so pleased.
| Piers Marrable? He writes his sevens like gallows. Everyone in this ward knows his hand; he signs everything, and he signs it large.
| My window looks over the inner ward. I see a great deal from it and I am asked about none of it.
| And if you are going up on the walk, which everybody seems to be today: my merlin slipped her jesses at Prime and sits on the south walk as if she had bought it. The sentry will not fetch her. A post is a post, he says.
: hawk-loose
? quest ladys-hawk at loose
% Lady Alys's merlin slipped her jesses at Prime and sits on the south walk by the Bakehouse Tower. Go up and look for her.
| Ffion. A merlin, a year to my hand, and she went off the sill at Prime with her jesses trailing and sat down on the south walk by the Bakehouse Tower as if she had bought it.
| I sent to the sentry. A post is a post, he says, and the King did not put him on the north walk to chase a lady's bird along the south one. He is right. I find I mind that he is right.
| Go up by the Bakehouse Tower door. If she is on the parapet do not reach for her; tell me where she sits and I will send the boy with the lure.
: hawk-seen
? quest ladys-hawk at seen
% You have been along the south walk by the Bakehouse Tower. Go back and tell Lady Alys what sits there.
| You went up. No, do not tell me: the parapet over the Bakehouse Tower door, which is the one place the boy cannot get a ladder to.
| She came back to the sill an hour after you went, on her own, as they do when the ward is quiet and nobody is looking at them. So you climbed for nothing, and I am obliged to you for it.
| You have seen the south walk by daylight, then, which most of this castle never has. It is a long way from the Bakehouse Tower door to anywhere, and nobody up there is written in any book.
: hawk-home
? quest ladys-hawk at home
% Ffion is back on her block. Whatever else is on the south walk is still there.
| She is on her block and sulking, and you went where the sentry would not. I do not forget that kind of thing.
| The wards are one ward up on that walk, clerk. I have said so before and nobody has asked me what I meant by it.
| My window looks over the inner ward. I see a great deal from it. Ask.
: window
? press default on walk-crosses
! says lady-window
| You have been up on the walk. Then you know what I know, that the wards are one ward up there.
| Last night: one lantern going up the Chapel Tower stair, late. And after, a shadow along the south walk, going westward, with no lantern at all.
| I did not think it my business. I have been married to a Constable eleven years.

@ inspector | Master Adam Fraunceys | The King's inspector | inner
: default
| The King's inspector has not dismounted yet.

